<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-vector-spaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vector Spaces</title>

  <section xml:id="sec-matrix-of-a-transformation">
    <title>The Matrix of a Transformation</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-matrix-of-S-P2-to-M22">
      <title>The matrix of a transformation <m>S : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{M}_{22}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B} = \{1, x, x^2\}</m> for <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>
          and an ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> for <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>,
          and find the matrix of the transformation <m>S</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The strategy is captured by the following diagram.
          The coordinate maps identify <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> with <m>\R^3</m>
          and <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> with <m>\R^4</m>,
          so the transformation <m>S</m> corresponds to a matrix transformation
          <m>\R^3 \to \R^4</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-commutative-diagram-S">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>S : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{M}_{22}</m>
            and the matrix transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^4</m> induced by the
            coordinate isomorphisms.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-commutative-diagram-S" width="55%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="commutative-diagram-S">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,240)" margins="5">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,10,6]">
                  <group at="commutative-square">
                    <label at="node-P2" anchor="(1.5,5)" alignment="center"><m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></label>
                    <label at="node-M22" anchor="(8.5,5)" alignment="center"><m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R3" anchor="(1.5,1)" alignment="center"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R4" anchor="(8.5,1)" alignment="center"><m>\mathbb{R}^4</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-S" endpoints="[(2.4,5),(7.3,5)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-S" anchor="(4.85,5.3)" alignment="north"><m>S</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-left-iso" endpoints="[(1.5,4.3),(1.5,1.7)]" arrows="2"/>
                    <line at="arrow-right-iso" endpoints="[(8.5,4.3),(8.5,1.7)]" arrows="2"/>

                    <line at="arrow-A" endpoints="[(2.2,1),(7.6,1)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-A" anchor="(4.9,0.45)" alignment="center">matrix transformation</label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="commutative-square"
                              text="A commutative square relating the transformation S to a matrix transformation">
                    <annotation ref="arrow-S" text="Across the top, S maps the polynomial space P 2 to the space of 2 by 2 matrices M 2 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-left-iso" text="On the left, the coordinate isomorphism identifies P 2 with R 3 in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-right-iso" text="On the right, the coordinate isomorphism identifies M 2 2 with R 4 in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-A" text="Across the bottom, a matrix transformation maps R 3 to R 4"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          At the level of the ordered bases, <m>S</m> sends the basis
          <m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m> of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> to the four matrices
          <m>\{M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3\}</m> spanning <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>,
          and the induced matrix transformation is given by a matrix <m>A</m>:
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-basis-diagram-S">
          <caption>
            The action of <m>S</m> on the ordered basis and the corresponding
            matrix <m>A</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-basis-diagram-S" width="55%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="basis-diagram-S">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,240)" margins="5">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,10,6]">
                  <group at="basis-square">
                    <label at="node-B" anchor="(1.5,5)" alignment="center"><m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-B1" anchor="(8.2,5)" alignment="center"><m>\{M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3\}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R3" anchor="(1.5,1)" alignment="center"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R4" anchor="(8.2,1)" alignment="center"><m>\mathbb{R}^4</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-S" endpoints="[(3.1,5),(6.1,5)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-S" anchor="(4.6,5.3)" alignment="north"><m>S</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-left" endpoints="[(1.5,4.3),(1.5,1.7)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <line at="arrow-right" endpoints="[(8.2,4.3),(8.2,1.7)]" arrows="1"/>

                    <line at="arrow-A" endpoints="[(2.2,1),(7.3,1)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-A" anchor="(4.75,1.3)" alignment="north"><m>A</m></label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="basis-square"
                              text="A commutative square showing the action of S on the ordered basis and the corresponding matrix A">
                    <annotation ref="arrow-S" text="Across the top, S sends the ordered basis 1, x, x squared to the four matrices M 0, M 1, M 2, M 3"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-left" text="On the left, the basis of P 2 is identified with coordinate vectors in R 3"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-right" text="On the right, the four matrices are identified with coordinate vectors in R 4"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-A" text="Across the bottom, the matrix A maps R 3 to R 4"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          After finding the output of the matrix transformation,
          we can use the isomorphism between <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> and <m>\R^4</m>
          and write the output as a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </section>

</chapter>
